    id: 'blog-56',
    title: 'How to Vet a Knitwear Factory: A Buyer\'s Checklist Before You Place an Order',
    excerpt: 'The due-diligence checklist for evaluating a knitwear manufacturer — legal identity, real production capability, references, audit documents, and the red flags that end sourcing relationships early.',
    content: 'Most sourcing disasters are visible before the first order is placed. The information is available — a business licence, a factory address, an audit report, a reference from another buyer — but it is only useful if someone actually checks it. Here is the checklist.\n\n## Stage 1: Verify the Legal Entity\n\nThe cheapest five minutes in sourcing:\n\n- **Business licence** — legal name, registration number, scope of business, registered capital. Ask for the document and confirm the name matches your contract.\n- **Name consistency** — the company name on the quote, the contract, and the bank account must match. A mismatch here is the single most important red flag in export sourcing.\n- **Export rights** — confirm the entity can legally export, or that your contract accounts for an export agent.\n\n**Why this matters:** paying a deposit to a different company name than the one you contracted removes most of your practical recourse if something goes wrong.\n\n## Stage 2: Confirm Real Production\n\nA trading company can be an excellent partner, but you should know which one you are buying from — and price it accordingly.\n\n| Question | Why it matters |\n|------------|-------------|\n| Do you own the knitting machines? | Determines schedule control |\n| How many machines, what gauge? | Determines real capacity and capability |\n| Can I see the production floor? | Video walkthrough or live call |\n| Which steps are in-house? | Knitting, linking, finishing, packing |\n| Who are your yarn suppliers? | Supply-chain depth and traceability |\n\n**Ask for a live video walkthrough**, not a photo gallery. Photos can be anyone\'s factory. A live call where you direct the camera to the knitting floor tells you what a brochure cannot.\n\n## Stage 3: Check Documentation\n\n- **Certifications:** Oeko-Tex for product safety, and social audits (BSCI or Sedex/SMETA) for retail vendor approval. Verify scope — a certificate covering one yarn is not a blanket claim.\n- **Audit dates:** audits expire. A two-year-old report describes the past.\n- **References:** ask for two or three existing clients in a comparable market segment. Then contact them.\n\n## Stage 4: Test With a Small Order\n\nDue diligence does not end at the vetting stage. The most reliable test of a factory is a small, complete order:\n\n1. Start with a **sample round** and judge responsiveness and accuracy.\n2. Place a **small trial order** — enough to reveal how the factory behaves under real production pressure.\n3. Judge on **communication quality**, not just final product. Problems happen at every factory; the variable is how they are handled.\n\nA factory that communicates well on a small order will usually communicate well on a large one. The reverse is also true.\n\n## The Red Flags\n\n| Red flag | Why it ends relationships |\n|------------|-------------|\n| Bank account name ≠ contract name | Removes practical recourse |\n| Refusal of a live factory walkthrough | Suggests no production to show |\n| No audit documents or vague answers | Retail compliance risk passes to you |\n| Pressure to skip sampling | Quality risk transferred to you |\n| 100% payment demanded upfront | Removes all buyer protection |\n| No verifiable references | No external evidence of delivery |\n\nAny single item deserves a pause. Two or more together describe a pattern.\n\n## FAQ\n\n**How do I verify a Chinese knitwear factory is real?**\nAsk for the business licence and confirm the legal name, then request a live video walkthrough of the production floor where you direct the camera. Confirm that the company name on the quote, contract and bank account all match — a mismatch is the most serious red flag.\n\n**Should I buy from a trading company or a manufacturer?**\nBoth can work. A manufacturer controls its own schedule and price structure; a trading company may offer wider product range and stronger project management. The essential requirement is knowing which you are dealing with, and verifying that whoever you contract can name and bind the actual production facility.\n\n**What certifications should a knitwear factory hold?**\nOeko-Tex STANDARD 100 for product safety is the most commonly requested, and BSCI or Sedex/SMETA social audits are standard vendor requirements for western retail. Always check the audit date and the certified scope, not just the logo.\n\n**How long should vetting take before placing an order?**\nLegal verification and a live walkthrough can be done in days. A sensible sequence is: verify entity, confirm production, check documents, then run a sample round and a small trial order before committing to volume production.\n\n## Sources\n\n- Standard export due-diligence practice for apparel sourcing (business licence, entity verification, banking consistency)\n- Knitwear certification schemes: Oeko-Tex STANDARD 100, BSCI (amfori), Sedex/SMETA\n\n## The Bottom Line\n\nVetting is not distrust — it is the routine that separates a supplier from a liability. Verify the entity, confirm real production with your own eyes, check documents and references, then test with a sample round and a small order. Five checks, all cheap, that prevent most sourcing failures before they start.\n\nLINFAIR is a Dalang-based knitwear manufacturer with in-house knitting, Oeko-Tex certified production, documented quality control at every stage, and clients across US, EU and UK markets. [Request a factory walkthrough](https://linfairwool.cn/contact) and we will show you the line.',
    date: 'September 21, 2026',
    category: 'Sourcing Guide',
    image: '/images/blog/blog-56-factory-audit.jpg',
